      "text": "The number of ways the coach can select a different team consists of eliminating the first player from every possibility of choosing two or more players.\n\nFor the many cases, use the combination composition. For each choice of the team, the coach always eliminates one of the remaining teammates (2 teams). This decreases the starting case by a factor of 2. Because the shoes are distinct, we must also have and initial arrangement.\n\nLet's plan our steps:\n1. Choose the first player.\n    - There are 7 ways to choose a player.\n\n2. Proceed with the remaining players by removing the first player.\n    - We now have 6 players to choose from.\n    - There are 6 ways to choose a different player.\n\n3. The number of ways to choose players is the multiplication of the first steps.\n    - There are 7 players to start, and the next players are 6, resulting in 7 * 6 ways.\n\n4. Before distributing the final picks among the team, the coach considers the order.\n    - If all players have their spots, the number of different distinct 3-player possibilities is 7 * 6 * 5 ways.\n\nAs such, Johnny can create 210 different distinct teams or games in his bag. The final answer is \\boxed{210}.",
